{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cggw1phghvaT5p6Qy5QmJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ I get **Dq = 2155 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 657 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 3700 cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 5.6)**. Dq and B come from exact formulas. C relies on an approximate formula for the ²E level and is less reliable.\n\n**1. Ligand-field splitting (from the ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₂g band)**\n\nFor d³ in an octahedral field, the energy of ⁴T₂g above the ground state is exactly 10Dq.\n- 10Dq = 21 550 cm⁻¹\n- **Dq = 2155 cm⁻¹**\n\n**2. Racah B (from the ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₁g(F) band)**\n\nThe exact expression, with Δ = 10Dq, is:\n\nE(⁴T₁g) = 1.5Δ + 7.5B − ½√(225B² + Δ² − 18ΔB)\n\n- Setting E = 28 500 cm⁻¹ gives 3825 + 7.5B = ½√(225B² + Δ² − 18ΔB).\n- Squaring both sides: (7650 + 15B)² = 225B² + Δ² − 18ΔB.\n- The B² terms cancel, leaving 617 400 B = 405 880 000.\n- **B ≈ 657 cm⁻¹**\n\nThis is about 72 % of the free-ion value for Cr³⁺ (about 918 cm⁻¹), so the nephelauxetic ratio β ≈ 0.72.\n\n**3. Racah C (from the spin-forbidden ⁴A₂g → ²Eg band)**\n\nI used the strong-field expression with a second-order correction for mixing with other configurations:\n\nE(²E) ≈ 9B + 3C − 90B²/Δ\n\n- 15 200 = 5917 + 3C − 1805\n- 3C ≈ 11 090\n- **C ≈ 3700 cm⁻¹**\n\nC can only be found from this band, and its value depends on the approximation. With no correction (E = 9B + 3C), C would be about 3090 cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 4.7). Diagonalising the full ²E energy matrix would give a more exact value; I didn't do that, but I'd expect it to land close to the corrected result.\n\n{\"Dq\": 2155, \"B\": 657, \"C\": 3700, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq and B are exact from the quartet bands; C comes from the 2E band using an approximate second-order formula.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":820,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":820,"ephem